+\emph{2) Weight Alignment and Normalization:}
+Pixel Shuffle rearranges the predicted weights onto the higher-resolution grid, and Softmax normalizes the $k^2$ weights at each output location:
+\begin{equation}
+K=\operatorname{Softmax}\!\left(\operatorname{PS}(K')\right).
+\label{eq:cafr-kernel}
+\end{equation}
+Softmax is applied along the $k^2$ neighborhood dimension. The resulting weight tensor is $K\in\mathbb{R}^{k^2\times sH\times sW}$, with each output location assigned $k^2$ normalized weights for local feature reassembly.
+
+\emph{3) Feature Reassembly:}
+Unfold extracts a $k\times k$ neighborhood around each input position. Nearest-neighbor upsampling then replicates the extracted neighborhoods to align them with the output resolution. The output is obtained by multiplying the predicted weights with the corresponding local features and summing over the $k^2$ neighborhood positions:
+\begin{equation}
+Y=\sum_{u=1}^{k^2}K_u\odot P_u.
+\label{eq:cafr-reassembly}
+\end{equation}
+Here, $K_u\in\mathbb{R}^{sH\times sW}$ and $P_u\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times sH\times sW}$ denote the predicted weight map and the aligned local feature map for the $u$-th neighborhood position, respectively. The symbol $\odot$ denotes element-wise multiplication, with the weights broadcast across feature channels. The resulting output is $Y\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times sH\times sW}$. Nearest-neighbor upsampling is used only for neighborhood alignment; content-adaptive weighted aggregation produces the final output.
